    <p class="stat-source">
      Source: OCP Medical Use of Cannabis Program 2025 Annual Report (Feb 13, 2026) reporting on 22 M.R.S. §2430-N requirements. Patient-certification counts include reprints (per OCP methodology); "registrant-related employment" series starts at 4,358 in 2024 because the registry identification card (RIC) two-year-cycle reform took effect July 1, 2024.
    </p>

    <h3>What the medical numbers show</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Dispensaries grew fastest.</strong> Registered medical dispensaries grew from 35 (2022) → 60 (2023) → 70 (2024) → 90 (2025), a 28.5% YoY expansion in 2025 alone — and 46 → 62 retail locations, +34.7% YoY. The 2025 expansion was fueled by caregivers who transitioned to formal dispensary status after the January 2021 dispensary-cap expiration and the PL 2023 c. 365 framework.</li>
      <li><strong>Patient volume is steady, not surging.</strong> Certifications grew 1.8% YoY in 2025 — slower than 2024 (+1.4%) but still positive. Compare this to the mature, flat-to-declining patient-count patterns in mature markets like Colorado or Oregon.</li>
      <li><strong>The caregiver population is shrinking.</strong> 1,539 active caregivers at year-end 2025 vs 1,677 in 2024 (−8.2%) — the seventh consecutive year of decline since the 2021 peak of 3,257. The caregiver exodus reflects PL 2023 c. 365 + PL 2023 c. 679 framework tightening, plus caregiver-to-dispensary transitions.</li>
      <li><strong>Provider count continues to climb.</strong> 808 active certifying providers in 2025 vs 777 in 2024 — a 3.9% increase, smaller than prior years. Providers remain concentrated: the top 10 (out of 808) issued 83,636 of 112,547 certifications (74.3% of all printed certifications statewide in 2025).</li>
    </ul>

    <Callout title="Caregiver context" type="note">
      286 registered caregivers reported operating a caregiver retail store in 2025 (MMCP 2025 Annual Report §4.2). Combined with the 62 registered medical-dispensary retail locations, Maine now has <strong>348 storefront-style medical retail points of sale</strong> — vs 187 adult-use retail stores. This makes the regulated medical side structurally larger than the adult-use side by storefront count, even though adult-use gross revenue ($246.4M) clearly exceeds the medical side's retail share.
    </Callout>
  </section>

