  <section id="tax-reset">
    <h2>January 1, 2026 Tax Reset</h2>

    <p>The biennial budget passed by the 131st Legislature and signed by Governor Mills (P.L. 2025, ch. 388) restructured Maine cannabis taxes effective January 1, 2026. The directional change: lower cultivation excise (30–43% reductions) financed by a higher retail sales tax (10.0% → 14.0%). Medical patients continue to pay only the 5.5% state sales tax on harvested cannabis, unchanged.</p>

    <table class="year-table" aria-label="Maine cannabis tax regime changes effective January 1 2026">
      <caption>Table 7 — Maine cannabis tax rates before and after the Jan 1, 2026 reset. Source: Maine Morning Star (Dec 31, 2025); Maine Revenue Services; P.L. 2025, ch. 388.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Tax Item</th>
          <th class="num-col">Before</th>
          <th class="num-col">After (Jan 1, 2026)</th>
          <th class="num-col">Change</th>
          <th>Source</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {taxDelta.map(r => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.item}</td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.before}</td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.after}</td>
            <td class="num-col">{r.delta}</td>
            <td>{r.source}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="stat-source">
      Source: Maine Morning Star (December 31, 2025) reporting the biennial budget; Maine Department of Administrative and Financial Services fiscal note; cross-checked against the Federation of Tax Administrators State Taxation and Sales of Recreational Marijuana tracker. The fiscal-note estimate: $15.4M in additional two-year sales-tax revenue (~$5.2M annualized), partially offset by $10.2M in lost two-year excise revenue (~$5.1M annualized). Net biennial revenue impact: ~$5.2M positive to the General Fund.
    </p>

    <h3>What this means in practice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>For consumers:</strong> A gram that retailed at $6.62 in December 2025 retails at roughly $6.86 in 2026 (4.0 pp × pretax price / 1.10) — a 3.6% effective shelf-price increase, unless operators absorb the sales-tax difference to keep shelf prices flat.</li>
      <li><strong>For cultivators:</strong> Excise tax per pound on flower drops from $335 to $223 — a 33.4% reduction. For a cultivator moving 100 lb/month, that is $11,200/month in saved excise (annualized ~$134,400).</li>
      <li><strong>For processors:</strong> Trim excise drops 33.0%; mature plants 34.3%; immature plants 33.3%; seeds 42.9%. Across a typical product mix, the per-pound effective rate drops roughly a third — meaningful relief for vertically integrated operators who carry the excise on their own wholesale-to-retail handoff.</li>
      <li><strong>For medical patients:</strong> No change. Patients continue to pay only the 5.5% state sales tax on harvested cannabis, with the 8% rate on edible cannabis products (classified as prepared food), unchanged. The MMCP 2025 Annual Report (§6) attributes the 5.2% YoY decline in medical sales tax revenue to a reweighting of sales channels toward caregiver-to-patient transfers (which are tax-exempt), not to a sales decline.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="trends">
    <h2>2026 Trends &amp; Outlook</h2>

    <h3>1. The plateau is the new normal</h3>
    <p>Maine's adult-use market is no longer in a growth-at-all-costs phase. Total 2026 sales are projected at $244–248M (H1 2026 actual + OCP reporting cadence) — within 1% of 2025. The pricing story tells the real story: average flower price has fallen from $11.85/gram (2021) to $6.62/gram (2025) and now to $6.10/gram YTD in 2026 — a 48.5% five-year decline on the most-purchased SKU. Operators have absorbed both price compression AND the Jan 1, 2026 sales-tax uplift. The survivor is the operator with a tight cost structure and a loyal patient/consumer base; the loser is anyone who grew on the assumption of continued double-digit top-line growth.</p>

    <h3>2. Consolidation is accelerating</h3>
    <p>Adult-use retail storefronts have been flat (187 active at year-end 2025); the OCP live roster shows {liveOcp.auRetailStores} active entries as of {liveOcp.asOf}. MJBizDaily, Cannabismarketcap, and the Cannabusinessplans Maine-market report all describe the national 2026 picture as "consolidation, maturation, and a pivot from growth-at-all-costs to sustainable profitability." Maine-specific consolidations: multi-store operators absorbing single-location ones, branded cultivation networks acquiring independent grows, and a small but real wave of opt-out operators shutting down. The market-pulse-2026 page's M&amp;A tracker captures the public transactions.</p>

    <h3>3. The Jan 1, 2026 tax restructure cuts two ways</h3>
    <p>The 14.0% retail sales tax (up from 10.0%) is a ~3.6% effective shelf-price uplift — modest, but on top of continued wholesale flower-price compression it tightens retail margins further. The 33.4% cultivation excise cut (flower $335 → $223/lb) returns some margin to growers and vertically integrated retailers. Net effect: a slight rebalancing from retail margin to wholesale margin, with Maine's smallest standalone dispensaries (those most exposed to retail-margin compression) the most at risk.</p>

    <h3>4. April 2026: testing-reform relief</h3>
    <p>P.L. 2025, ch. 764 (emergency effective April 19, 2026) eliminates mandatory contaminant testing for adult-use edible products manufactured from concentrate that has already passed the required contaminant panel. Edibles still require THC-potency, cannabinoid-content, and homogeneity testing. This is a meaningful manufacturing-friction reduction for processors in the edibles-and-beverages category — a segment BDSA projects at +14.6% CAGR nationally through 2031.</p>

    <h3>5. The medical program is the structural counterweight</h3>
    <p>While adult-use plateaued, medical dispensaries grew 28.5% YoY in 2025 (70 → 90), medical retail locations grew 34.7% YoY (46 → 62), and medical employees grew 21.4% YoY (4,358 → 5,291). Patient certifications remain the soft metric (+1.8% YoY in 2025 vs +1.4% in 2024). The discrepancy — fast store-front growth on slow patient-certification growth — suggests the medical-channel expansion is being driven by caregiver-to-dispensary transitions and new-product-format adoption among existing patients, not by a surge of new patients. The OCP itself recommends (in the MMCP 2025 Annual Report §7.2) the Legislature establish mandatory contaminant testing and inventory tracking in the medical program "to ensure patient health and safety," a recommendation that has been carried over in successive sessions and remains OCP's top legislative ask.</p>

    <h3>6. Outlook: dollars flat, transactions slightly up, active operator count slightly down</h3>
    <p>The 2026 picture is consistent with the 2025 plateau: total dollars essentially flat ($244–248M annualized), total transactions up modestly (+3–5% YoY), and active operator count down (~10–15 stores closed or license-lapsed net of new openings through year-end). The state's bid to consolidate the caregiver gray market into the regulated dispensary channel (via continued dispensary-cap expiration effects and caregiver-to-dispensary transitions) is the structural offset: it keeps supply-chain-side activity elevated while AU-side dollar revenue stays flat. <strong>For operators, planning on continued growth at this point is planning on a different market than the one the data describes.</strong></p>
  </section>

