  <section id="methodology" class="methodology">
    <h3>Methodology &amp; Sources</h3>
    <p><strong>Source precedence.</strong> Where two sources disagree, we use: (1) the OCP annual reports (28-B M.R.S. §113 for adult-use; 22 M.R.S. §2430-N for medical) as the primary source; (2) Maine Revenue Services (MRS) monthly/quarterly cannabis-taxable-sales reports for monthly cadence; (3) the OCP live licensee-search CSV (refreshed monthly) for current-month operating footprint; (4) mainecannabis.org, Marijuana Herald, MJBizDaily, and MaineBiz for journalism date-stamps on data releases; (5) MDG's own Opt-In Tracker and licensee aggregates for municipal-level data not yet covered by OCP.</p>
    <p><strong>Aggregate spend definition.</strong> "Combined sales" on this page is defined as adult-use retail sales (OCP-reported) + medical retail sales (MRS-reportable to the General Fund; excludes caregiver-to-patient tax-exempt transfers). The two streams are reported on different fiscal bases: adult-use reports on calendar-year cadence (Jan 1 – Dec 31), while the medical sales tax figures are reported by Maine fiscal year (July 1 – June 30) per MRS reporting. Direct apples-to-apples monthly comparison is therefore only available on an annualized basis.</p>
    <p><strong>Active vs operating.</strong> "Active" retailer counts reflect the OCP Annual Report tally (Dec 31 snapshot). The "live OCP roster" figure is the current month's OCP licensee-search CSV deduped by store name + license number. The two figures are not directly comparable — the Annual Report is year-end, the live CSV is today.</p>
    <p><strong>MRS vs OCP numbers.</strong> For some months and some categories, MRS-reported adult-use taxable sales diverge from OCP-reported adult-use retail sales by 1–3%. The difference reflects (a) timing of tax remittance vs dispatch, (b) the small but non-zero caregiver-channel that pays sales tax but not excise, and (c) returns/credits. Where the two diverge materially we note it; for primary citations we anchor on OCP.</p>
    <p><strong>Refresh cadence.</strong> This page is refreshed when (a) a new OCP annual report is published (typically Q1 following the report year) and (b) when a substantial structural event shifts an underlying assumption. The big events to watch: an OCP 2026 Annual Report (expected Feb 2027), further biennial budget tax changes (next opportunity 2027 session), any OCP renewal cohort processing.</p>
